CH[24] = dict(
title='Chapter 24. Transistor Datasheets in SPICE: BJT, MOSFET, and JFET',
open="A 2N3904 model can reproduce the transistor's gain at 10 mA within a few percent and still be the wrong model for your design, because your design runs the transistor at 20 µA, at 85 °C, or switching in 30 ns.",
where="Discrete transistor circuits, gate-driver and power-stage simulation, thermal design, and every board-level design review that leans on a vendor model.",
blocks=[
('P', "The method of Chapter 23 carries over unchanged: map each parameter to a measurement, fit in an order that keeps them apart, validate on data the fit never saw, and record the range. What changes is the number of parameters and the number of ways a transistor can leave its fitted range."),
('H2', '24.1 The bipolar transistor: Gummel-Poon from a data sheet'),
('P', "SPICE's bipolar model is the Gummel-Poon model of 1970. Its core is the transport of Chapter 8: a collector current Is exp(VBE/VT), a forward gain Bf, and a reverse gain Br. To that it adds what Chapter 9 measured: the Early voltage Vaf; a second, non-ideal base current with saturation current Ise and emission coefficient Ne, which lowers β at low current; and a knee current Ikf beyond which high injection lowers β again. Rb, Re, and Rc add the ohmic resistances; Cje, Cjc, Vje, Vjc, Mje, and Mjc the junction capacitances; Tf the forward transit time that sets fT, with Itf, Vtf, and Xtf shaping its growth at high current; and Tr the reverse transit time that sets the storage time when the transistor saturates. Xtb and Xti carry β and Is over temperature."),
('P', "A widely distributed 2N3904 card reads Is = 6.734 fA, Bf = 416.4, Ne = 1.259, Ise = 6.734 fA, Ikf = 66.78 mA, Vaf = 74.03 V, Br = 0.7371, Rb = 10 Ω, Rc = 1 Ω, Cje = 4.493 pF, Cjc = 3.638 pF, Tf = 301.2 ps, and Tr = 239.5 ns, plus temperature and shaping parameters. A Bf of 416 is not the β you will measure. It is the ceiling that the Ise term pulls down at low current and the Ikf term pulls down at high current. The data sheet's hFE limits are what the card must respect: at VCE = 1 V, a minimum of 40 at 0.1 mA, 70 at 1 mA, 100 to 300 at 10 mA, 60 at 50 mA, and 30 at 100 mA."),
('WE', 'Worked Example 24.1', "Simulate the card's hFE at VCE = 1 V at the five data sheet currents and compare it with the limits. Then find the upper bound on fT that Tf alone sets.",
 "ngspice gives about 96, 133, 160, 130, and 100 at 0.1, 1, 10, 50, and 100 mA (Figure 24.1). Every value is inside the limits, and the peak near 10 mA sits where the data sheet places its 100-to-300 window. Tf alone gives fT ≤ 1/(2π × 301.2 ps) = 528 MHz; the junction capacitances, charged through 1/gm, lower it further. The data sheet guarantees at least 300 MHz at 10 mA and 20 V. The card describes a typical part, as a library card should. A design that needs gain must still work with the minimum, 100 at 10 mA: scale Bf down for a worst-case run or, better, bias so that gain does not matter, as Chapter 9 advised. Below about 10 µA, where the data sheet gives no limits at all, Ise and Ne are extrapolating; measure if your circuit lives there."),
('FIG', 'b3s_2n3904.png', "Figure 24.1 — hFE of the 2N3904 library card of Worked Example 24.1 at VCE = 1 V, simulated in ngspice, against the data sheet's minimum values and the 300 maximum at 10 mA. The card is a typical part, inside every limit.", "Semilog plot of hFE against collector current rising from about 96 at 0.1 milliampere to a peak of 160 near 10 milliamperes and falling to 100 at 100 milliamperes, above red triangles marking the minimum limits."),
('P', "VBIC and HICUM, the successors to Gummel-Poon for RF and SiGe transistors, add self-heating, a parasitic substrate transistor, and a better transit-time model. A foundry's SiGe PDK uses one of them; a discrete small-signal transistor is usually well served by Gummel-Poon inside the data sheet's range."),
('H2', '24.2 The MOSFET: level 1, and why BSIM exists'),
('P', "Chapter 10's square law is SPICE's level 1 MOSFET: a threshold Vto, a transconductance parameter Kp (μCox, multiplied by W/L on the instance line), the channel-length modulation Lambda, and the body-effect pair Gamma and Phi. For a discrete MOSFET with W and L set to 1, Kp is the whole device's K, and three data sheet numbers fix the model."),
('WE', 'Worked Example 24.2', "Fit a level 1 model to the onsemi 2N7000 typical values: VGS(th) = 2.1 V at ID = 1 mA with VDS = VGS; forward transconductance 320 mS at ID = 200 mA; RDS(on) = 1.2 Ω at VGS = 10 V and 0.5 A. Then compare the fitted model with the data sheet's typical 1.8 Ω at VGS = 4.5 V and 75 mA, and its typical on-state current of 600 mA at VGS = 4.5 V and VDS = 10 V.",
 "In saturation gm = √(2 K ID), so K = gm²/(2 ID) = 0.32²/0.4 = 0.256 A/V². The threshold criterion puts 1 mA at 2.1 V: 1 mA = (K/2)(2.1 − Vto)², so 2.1 − Vto = √(2×10⁻³/0.256) = 0.088 V and Vto = 2.01 V. The data sheet's threshold is not the model's Vto; it is the gate voltage at which a stated current flows. In the linear region at 10 V the channel alone gives 1/(K(VGS − Vto)) = 1/(0.256 × 7.99) = 0.49 Ω against 1.2 Ω measured, and the remaining 0.71 Ω goes into a drain resistance Rd. With Rd, ngspice reproduces 1.04 mA at 2.1 V and 1.21 Ω at 10 V. At 4.5 V it gives 2.31 Ω against the typical 1.8 Ω, 28 % high, and 0.79 A of on-state current against the typical 0.6 A. Three numbers fixed three parameters exactly; the two the fit did not use show the square law's limits. Real mobility falls as the gate field rises, so a single K cannot match both the low-drive and the high-drive end."),
('P', "That failure, multiplied by every short-channel effect of Chapter 11, is why BSIM exists: mobility degradation, velocity saturation, drain-induced barrier lowering, and dozens more effects, each with parameters fitted across sizes and temperatures. Nobody fits BSIM by hand; it arrives with the PDK. A discrete power MOSFET gets something in between, a subcircuit."),
('H2', '24.3 The power MOSFET subcircuit'),
('P', "A power MOSFET data sheet carries much more than a threshold and an on-resistance, and every line has a test condition that matters. VGS(th) is quoted at a small current, 250 µA for the IRFZ44N, so it says nothing about the gate voltage needed for full current. RDS(on) is quoted at one gate voltage, one drain current, and 25 °C, 17.5 mΩ maximum at 10 V and 25 A for this part, and its curves against VGS and temperature show how fast it grows, often by 1.5 to 2 times from 25 to 150 °C in silicon. The forward transconductance gfs is quoted at a large current, 19 S minimum at 25 A. The capacitances Ciss, Coss, and Crss are measured at VGS = 0 and a stated VDS, here 1470, 360, and 88 pF typical at 25 V and 1 MHz, and all three fall steeply as VDS rises, Crss most of all."),
('P', "The gate-charge curve tells the switching story in one figure (Figure 24.2). With the gate driven by a constant current, VGS first rises while the input capacitance charges, Qgs. At the plateau the drain voltage falls, and the whole gate current goes into the gate-drain capacitance, the Miller effect of Chapter 13 made visible: VGS stays flat while Qgd flows. Then the gate charges on to the drive voltage, completing Qg. The IRFZ44N data sheet gives Qg = 63 nC, Qgs = 14 nC, and Qgd = 23 nC, all maximum values at ID = 25 A, VDS = 44 V, and VGS = 10 V."),
('P', "A level 1 MOSFET cannot hold all of this, so vendors ship a subcircuit: a MOSFET core, often level 1 or level 3, with resistances for the package and the drift region, voltage-dependent capacitors for Cgd and Cds (sometimes diodes used as capacitors), and a separate body diode with its own Is, N, Rs, Tt, and BV. Some models add a thermal node whose voltage is the junction temperature, so that RDS(on) rises as the device heats. LTspice's VDMOS element packs much of this into one model line. Read the subcircuit's header: a good vendor states which curves were fitted, and at which temperature."),
('WE', 'Worked Example 24.3', "A gate driver with a 10 V output drives the IRFZ44N through a total gate resistance of 12 Ω, the data sheet's switching test value. Assume the Miller plateau sits at 5.0 V (an assumed value for this example; read the real one from the data sheet's gate-charge figure). Estimate the time the drain voltage takes to fall, the gate energy per cycle, and the gate-drive power at 100 kHz.",
 "On the plateau the gate current is (10 − 5)/12 = 0.42 A, so the drain transition takes Qgd/Ig = 23 nC/0.42 A = 55 ns, in line with the data sheet's typical rise time of 60 ns measured with the same 12 Ω (VDD = 28 V, ID = 25 A). Each cycle the driver delivers Qg × V = 63 nC × 10 V = 0.63 µJ, all of it dissipated in the driver and the gate resistance over one turn-on and one turn-off. At 100 kHz that is 63 mW. Every number used the maximum Qg, so the estimate is conservative; the plateau voltage, which moves with drain current and temperature, is the number to read carefully."),
('FIG', 'b3s_gatecharge.png', "Figure 24.2 — Gate charge of the IRFZ44N from its data sheet maxima, Qgs = 14 nC, Qgd = 23 nC, and Qg = 63 nC at 10 V, with the plateau voltage assumed at 5.0 V for Worked Example 24.3.", "Gate voltage against gate charge rising linearly to a flat plateau at 5 volts between 14 and 37 nanocoulombs, then rising to 10 volts at 63 nanocoulombs."),
('P', "The body diode and the safe operating area mark the subcircuit's limits. The body diode's reverse recovery, which matters in every half bridge, is fitted with Tt and sometimes with a separate recovery model; check it against the data sheet's Qrr and trr at the stated di/dt. The safe operating area, the region of current and voltage the device survives for a given pulse length, is not in the model at all. A simulator will run a MOSFET at 50 A and 40 V for a millisecond without complaint; the data sheet's SOA curve, not the simulation, says whether the part survives."),
('H2', '24.4 Temperature, TNOM, and thermal networks'),
('P', "Every model card is fitted at a nominal temperature, TNOM, which is 27 °C by default in SPICE. The simulator scales Is with Eg and Xti, β with Xtb, and resistances with linear and quadratic coefficients when the card provides them. If the vendor fitted at 25 °C and the card does not say so, the 2 K difference is harmless. If the card has no temperature parameters and you simulate at 125 °C, the result is a 25 °C device in a 125 °C simulation."),
('P', "Junction temperature itself comes from a thermal network. The data sheet's transient thermal impedance curve Zth(t) shows how the junction heats after a power step, and vendors fit it with a Foster network, a chain of RC pairs whose responses add, Zth(t) = Σ Ri(1 − exp(−t/τi)) with τi = Ri Ci. A Foster network fits the curve, but its internal nodes have no physical meaning, so it cannot be extended with a heat sink. A Cauer network, a ladder of series resistances and shunt capacitances to ground, has nodes that correspond to physical layers, die, solder, and case, and can be joined to a heat-sink model. Both are ordinary circuits in SPICE, with watts as amperes and kelvin as volts."),
('EQ', 'Zth(t) = Σ Ri [1 − exp(−t/τi)],   ΔTj = P × Zth(t)'),
('WE', 'Worked Example 24.4', "A Foster fit to a transistor's Zth curve has two pairs, R1 = 0.4 K/W with τ1 = 0.5 ms and R2 = 1.2 K/W with τ2 = 10 ms (example values, 1.6 K/W in total to the case). Find the junction temperature rise above the case for a single 1 ms pulse of 50 W and for a continuous 50 W.",
 "Zth(1 ms) = 0.4 × (1 − e⁻²) + 1.2 × (1 − e^(−0.1)) = 0.4 × 0.865 + 1.2 × 0.095 = 0.346 + 0.114 = 0.460 K/W, so ΔT = 50 × 0.460 = 23 K. Continuous: 50 × 1.6 = 80 K. A 1 ms pulse sees less than a third of the steady thermal resistance, which is why the SOA allows far more power in short pulses. The 1.6 K/W total matches the IRFZ44N's rating, 94 W at a 25 °C case with a 175 °C maximum junction, (175 − 25)/94 = 1.6 K/W; the two time constants are example values, not that part's."),
('H2', '24.5 JFETs and the special diodes'),
('P', "SPICE's JFET is a square law with three main parameters: Vto, the pinch-off voltage (negative for an n-channel part), Beta, the transconductance parameter, and Lambda. The saturation current is ID = Beta (VGS − Vto)², so the data sheet's IDSS, the current at VGS = 0, gives Beta = IDSS/Vto². JFET data sheets state IDSS and VGS(off) as wide ranges: for the J310, IDSS from 24 to 60 mA and VGS(off) from −2.0 to −6.5 V. A single card is one point inside that box, and a circuit that depends on IDSS needs a trim or a bias that does not care."),
('WE', 'Worked Example 24.5', "Fit Beta for a J310-like part with IDSS = 40 mA and Vto = −4 V, a point inside the data sheet ranges, and find ID and gm at VGS = −1 V. Then find ID at the same bias for the corner parts IDSS = 24 mA with Vto = −2 V and IDSS = 60 mA with Vto = −6.5 V.",
 "Beta = 0.040/16 = 2.5 mA/V². At VGS = −1 V, ID = 2.5×10⁻³ × (−1 + 4)² = 22.5 mA, and gm = 2 Beta (VGS − Vto) = 2 × 2.5×10⁻³ × 3 = 15 mS. The first corner has Beta = 0.024/4 = 6 mA/V² and ID = 6×10⁻³ × 1² = 6 mA; the second has Beta = 0.060/42.25 = 1.42 mA/V² and ID = 1.42×10⁻³ × 5.5² = 43 mA. The same fixed bias gives anywhere from 6 to 43 mA across the data sheet box, which is why JFET bias circuits use source resistors or current sources rather than a fixed gate voltage."),
('P', "The special diodes reuse the level 1 diode card with the emphasis moved. A Zener is a diode whose BV and Ibv carry the design, with the breakdown slope setting the rz of Chapter 6; the temperature coefficient of the Zener voltage needs its own parameter or a subcircuit. An LED is a level 1 diode with a large N and an Is small enough to put its forward voltage near 2 to 3 V; its light output is not in the model, so a behavioral source proportional to current models the optical side. A Schottky diode has a large Is, an N near 1.0 to 1.1, almost no Tt because it stores no minority charge, and a strong temperature dependence of leakage, set by giving Eg the value of the barrier height. The op-amp, which needs a macromodel rather than a device card, is treated in Book 2, Circuits, Components, and Control, Sections 15.7, 15.8, and 16.8."),
('KEY', "Key idea. Each transistor card is a fit to the data sheet's typical curves at their test conditions. Check it at the data sheet's own points, know which condition each number was measured under, and add from the data sheet what the card cannot carry: worst case, temperature, and the safe operating area."),
('P', "Part VIII has turned data sheets into models and models into honest estimates. Part IX widens the range of devices: the materials beyond silicon, the devices that make and detect light, the sensors, and the economics of building a chip at all."),
],
bench="Before a model enters a design review, simulate the data sheet's own test circuits with it: the hFE sweep, the threshold condition, the gate-charge circuit, and the switching test with its stated gate resistance. If the model cannot reproduce the page it came from, nothing it says about your circuit is worth a margin. Then simulate once more at the hot corner with the minimum gain or the maximum on-resistance, because the typical card is the part you will not always get.",
job="A characterization lab is where these models start. Its engineers run source-measure units, curve tracers, capacitance meters, gate-charge and switching testers, and thermal-impedance setups, usually in automated sequences that sweep dozens of parts over temperature overnight and log every point with its test condition. Much of that automation is written in LabVIEW and sequenced with TestStand; " + B9 + ", covers both in its chapter \"LabVIEW and TestStand: Graphical Test for the Characterization Lab\", and the instruments in \"Source-Measure Units and Parametric Analyzers\". The lab's output is the data sheet's typical curves, the minimum and maximum limits from statistics over many lots, and the raw data from which the part's SPICE models are fitted.",
practice=[
"A Gummel-Poon card has Tf = 400 ps. Find the upper bound on fT that Tf alone sets.",
"A small MOSFET has gm = 100 mS at ID = 50 mA in saturation. Find the level 1 K.",
"The MOSFET of Problem 2 has a data sheet threshold of 1.8 V at 250 µA. Find its level 1 Vto.",
"A gate driver delivers 1.5 A on the Miller plateau to a MOSFET with Qgd = 30 nC. Find the drain-voltage transition time.",
"With the Foster network of Worked Example 24.4, find the junction temperature rise for a single 10 ms pulse of 30 W.",
"A JFET has IDSS = 10 mA and Vto = −2.5 V. Find Beta and ID at VGS = −1 V.",
],
wyau=(["Bf is a ceiling; hFE comes from Bf, Ise, Ne, and Ikf together.",
       "A data sheet threshold is a current criterion, not the model's Vto.",
       "Gate charge: transition time = Qgd/Igate on the plateau; drive power = Qg V f.",
       "Junction temperature: ΔTj = P Zth(t) from a Foster fit."],
      ["VBIC, HICUM, and BSIM parameter sets.", "Converting a Foster network into a Cauer ladder."]),
)
CH[25] = dict(
title='Chapter 25. Materials for Speed, Power, and Heat',
open="Why does a 5G base station use gallium nitride, a phone's antenna switch silicon on insulator, a satellite receiver indium phosphide, while laboratories chase gallium oxide and diamond? Each crystal wins a different contest, and a handful of material constants say which.",
where="Choosing an RF or power technology, reading foundry offerings, evaluating wide-bandgap power devices, and judging research news about ultrawide-bandgap materials.",
blocks=[
('H2', '25.1 The constants that decide'),
('P', "Five material properties carry most of the decision. The bandgap Eg sets the intrinsic carrier density and so the highest operating temperature, and through the critical field it sets the voltage a given thickness can block. The critical field Ec, which rises roughly as the square of the gap, sets breakdown. The electron mobility μ sets on-resistance and low-field speed, and the saturation velocity vsat sets how fast carriers cross a short channel at high field. The thermal conductivity k decides whether the heat that a small, powerful device makes can leave it."),
('P', "Representative room-temperature values follow, rounded, from Sze and Ng for the classic semiconductors and from the reviews of Tsao and colleagues (2018) and Woo and colleagues (2024) for the wide-gap and ultrawide-gap ones. These are orientation values, not design inputs; use the current datasheet or foundry process design kit for a specific device. Each line gives the gap, critical field, electron mobility, relative permittivity, saturation velocity, and thermal conductivity:"),
('B', "Silicon: 1.12 eV; 0.3 MV/cm; 1,400 cm²/V·s; 11.7; 1.0×10⁷ cm/s; about 150 W/m·K."),
('B', "Gallium arsenide: 1.42 eV; 0.4 MV/cm; 8,500 cm²/V·s; 12.9; about 1×10⁷ cm/s; about 55 W/m·K."),
('B', "Indium phosphide: 1.34 eV; 0.5 MV/cm; 5,400 cm²/V·s; 12.5; about 1×10⁷ cm/s; about 68 W/m·K."),
('B', "4H silicon carbide: 3.26 eV; 2.5 MV/cm; 900 cm²/V·s; 9.7; 2×10⁷ cm/s; about 370 W/m·K."),
('B', "Gallium nitride: 3.4 eV; 3.3 MV/cm; about 1,000 cm²/V·s in bulk and 1,500 to 2,000 in the two-dimensional gas; 10.4; 1.5 to 2×10⁷ cm/s; about 253 W/m·K."),
('B', "Beta gallium oxide: 4.8 to 4.9 eV; about 8 MV/cm; about 180 cm²/V·s; about 10; about 1.1×10⁷ cm/s; only 11 to 27 W/m·K, depending on direction."),
('B', "Diamond: 5.5 eV; about 10 MV/cm; up to about 4,500 cm²/V·s in the purest crystals; 5.7; about 2.3×10⁷ cm/s; 2,290 to 3,450 W/m·K, the highest of any material."),
('B', "Aluminum nitride: about 6.0 eV; about 15 MV/cm; about 430 cm²/V·s; about 9.8; about 1.3×10⁷ cm/s; about 285 to 319 W/m·K."),
('P', "The critical field depends on doping and on how breakdown is defined; mobility depends on doping, temperature, and crystal quality. The ultrawide-gap values, in particular, come from crystals and devices still under development. Use these values consistently in this book's comparisons, and replace them with the vendor's values for any real part. The silicon mobility here is the undoped value; the problems in Parts I to VII use the declared 1000 cm²/V·s."),
('H2', '25.2 Two figures of merit'),
('P', "Section 19.1 derived the ideal unipolar drift region and its specific on-resistance, Ron,sp = 4 BV²/(εs μn Ec³). Baliga's figure of merit is the denominator, εs μ Ec³: at a given blocking voltage, a material with a higher BFOM has a less resistive drift region. Johnson's figure of merit for RF power transistors, met in Section 7.1, is Ec vsat/2π: the highest frequency at which a transistor can still swing a given voltage, or equally, the voltage it can swing at a given frequency. Because Ec grows roughly as Eg², the Baliga figure grows roughly as the sixth power of the gap."),
('EQ', 'BFOM = εs μ Ec³,   JFOM = Ec vsat / 2π'),
('WE', 'Worked Example 25.1', "Compute the BFOM and JFOM of 4H-SiC, GaN, and β-Ga2O3 relative to silicon with the values of Section 25.1, and the ideal drift-region Ron,sp of each at 1200 V.",
 "With Ec in MV/cm, silicon's εr μ Ec³ is 11.7 × 1400 × 0.3³ = 442. SiC: 9.7 × 900 × 2.5³ = 136,400, 308 times silicon. GaN: 10.4 × 1000 × 3.3³ = 373,700, 845 times. Ga2O3: 10 × 180 × 8³ = 921,600, about 2,080 times. The JFOM ratio is the ratio of Ec vsat: SiC 2.5 × 2/0.3 = 16.7, GaN 3.3 × 2/0.3 = 22, and Ga2O3 8 × 1.1/0.3 = 29 (Figure 25.1). The ideal drift region at 1200 V comes to 0.48 mΩ·cm² for SiC, as in Worked Example 19.1, 0.17 for GaN, and 0.071 for Ga2O3, against about 150 for silicon at this section's mobility (Figure 25.2). Ga2O3 wins both ratings on paper; its thermal conductivity, less than a tenth of SiC's, is why it is not yet in your power supply."),
('FIG', 'b3s_fom.png', "Figure 25.1 — Baliga (top) and Johnson (bottom) figures of merit relative to silicon, computed from the constants of Section 25.1. The gap enters the Baliga figure roughly as its sixth power, which is why the ultrawide-gap materials stand so far above the rest.", "Two bar charts on log axes for eight materials; the Baliga chart rises from 1 for silicon to about 58,000 for diamond, the Johnson chart from 1 to about 77."),
('FIG', 'b3s_ronsp.png', "Figure 25.2 — The ideal unipolar drift-region limit of Section 19.1 for six materials. At 1200 V the line falls from about 150 mΩ·cm² in silicon to 0.48 in 4H-SiC, 0.17 in GaN, 0.07 in Ga2O3, and a few thousandths in AlN and diamond.", "Log-log plot of specific on-resistance against breakdown voltage with parallel lines for silicon, silicon carbide, gallium nitride, gallium oxide, aluminum nitride, and diamond, and a dotted line at 1200 volts."),
('KEY', "Key idea. Baliga's figure εμEc³ ranks materials for high-voltage switches, Johnson's Ec vsat for RF power. Both rise steeply with the gap; neither says anything about heat, cost, or whether the crystal can be doped and grown."),
('H2', '25.3 Silicon variants: SOI and SiGe'),
('P', "Two variants of silicon deserve their own place. Silicon on insulator, SOI, puts the active layer on a buried oxide. For RF switches the point is the substrate: a high-resistivity handle wafer with a trap-rich layer under the oxide keeps the substrate from becoming a lossy, nonlinear conductor, and stacking transistors in series lets a switch built from 2.5 V devices hold tens of volts of RF. Nearly every phone antenna switch, and many of its low-noise amplifiers, are RF SOI. Fully depleted SOI, FD-SOI, uses a silicon film so thin that the channel has no neutral body, which removes the floating-body effects and most junction capacitance, and it adds a back gate through the thin buried oxide that shifts the threshold by tens of millivolts per volt, a knob that low-power and millimeter-wave designs use. SOI's price is heat: the buried oxide conducts heat about a hundred times worse than silicon, so self-heating is larger."),
('P', "Silicon-germanium puts the heterojunction of Section 7.7 into the base of a bipolar transistor inside a CMOS-compatible process. Production SiGe BiCMOS offers transistors with fT and fmax in the hundreds of gigahertz next to CMOS logic, and research devices have passed 500 GHz fmax, which is why SiGe carries automotive radar at 77 GHz, optical-link drivers, and many millimeter-wave front ends."),
('WE', 'Worked Example 25.2', "An RF SOI antenna switch must hold a 20 V peak RF swing in its off state. Each transistor in the stack withstands 2.5 V. How many must be stacked for a 25 % margin, and what happens to the on-resistance?",
 "The stack must hold 20 × 1.25 = 25 V, so it needs 25/2.5 = 10 transistors in series. In the on state their resistances add, so each must be ten times wider than a single device would need for the same total on-resistance, and the off-state capacitance, ten small capacitors in series, falls accordingly. The product of on-resistance and off-capacitance, the switch figure of merit, is a property of the process, which is why RF SOI foundries compete on it."),
('H2', '25.4 InP, GaAs, and GaN as materials'),
('P', "Chapter 7 named the devices; here are the material reasons. GaAs and InP have light electrons with several times silicon's mobility and a high peak velocity, so their HEMTs and HBTs are fast at low voltage; their thermal conductivities, about a third and a half of silicon's, limit their power density. InP lattice-matches the InGaAs and InAlAs layers that give the highest-mobility channels and the narrow-gap photodetectors of Chapter 26, which is why one crystal serves both the 100 GHz amplifier and the 1550 nm receiver. GaN's polarization gives the two-dimensional gas without doping, its critical field gives the voltage, and its usual RF substrate, SiC at about 370 W/m·K, removes the heat. GaN on silicon trades some of that heat path and crystal quality for large, inexpensive wafers, which suits power conversion; GaN on SiC remains the choice for RF power."),
('H2', '25.5 The ultrawide-gap crystals: Ga2O3, diamond, and AlN'),
('P', "Gallium oxide has the attraction of melt growth: large single-crystal wafers can be pulled from a melt, as for silicon and sapphire, which SiC and GaN cannot do. Its n-type doping is controllable, and Schottky diodes and transistors blocking more than a kilovolt have been reported. It has two deep weaknesses. No usable p-type doping exists, so there is no pn junction, no conventional MOSFET body, and no bipolar device. And its thermal conductivity is the lowest in the list, so heat must leave through the top surface, through thinned wafers, or through a bonded layer of a better conductor."),
('P', "Diamond has the opposite profile: the best thermal conductivity of any material, a high critical field, and high mobilities for both carriers, but its dopants sit deep in the gap, boron about 0.37 eV above the valence band and phosphorus about 0.6 eV below the conduction band, so only a small fraction of them is ionized at room temperature. Devices use hydrogen-terminated surfaces, which form a hole channel without dopants, or very thin, heavily doped layers. Wafers are small and expensive. Diamond's most practical electronic role today is as a heat spreader under GaN, and its most exciting one is the quantum sensor of Chapter 27."),
('P', "Aluminum nitride, with a gap near 6 eV, has the highest critical field of the three and good thermal conductivity, and it is already a production material in another role, the piezoelectric film of Chapter 21. As a transistor material it appears mostly in AlGaN alloys and as a barrier or buffer layer in GaN devices, because making pure AlN conductive is hard. Ultraviolet LEDs for disinfection near 265 nm, a photon energy of 1240/265 = 4.7 eV, use AlGaN with a high aluminum content."),
('P', "Beyond these crystals, the research frontier includes two-dimensional semiconductors such as molybdenum disulfide, studied as channels for transistors beyond the nanosheet, and carbon nanotubes and graphene, whose remarkable mobilities have not yet become a manufacturable logic switch; graphene has no bandgap at all. Ferroelectric hafnium oxide, a relative of the high-κ gate dielectric of Chapter 20, has been demonstrated in embedded memories. Whether any of these reaches volume production is a question this book leaves open; the constants of Section 25.1 are the tool for judging the claims."),
('H2', '25.6 A map of RF and power technologies'),
('P', "Together, the materials divide the frequency-and-power plane into rough regions with wide overlaps. CMOS and FD-SOI integrate whole radios up to millimeter waves at milliwatts to about a watt. RF SOI supplies the switches and many low-noise amplifiers of every phone front end. SiGe BiCMOS covers radar and links from about 10 GHz to beyond 100 GHz at milliwatts to hundreds of milliwatts. GaAs HBTs and pHEMTs serve handset and Wi-Fi power amplifiers near a watt and low-noise functions to about 100 GHz. InP HEMTs and HBTs give the lowest noise and the highest speed, above about 100 GHz and in optical links. LDMOS covers base stations and industrial heating below about 4 GHz at tens to hundreds of watts, and GaN on SiC covers watts to kilowatts from about 1 to 40 GHz in radar, base stations, and satellites. For power conversion, Chapter 19 placed silicon MOSFETs and IGBTs, SiC up to 3.3 kV and beyond, and GaN on silicon from 100 to 900 V. Book 4 designs the circuits that live in each region."),
('P', "The materials decide what is possible. Chapter 26 shows what some of them do with light."),
],
bench="When a vendor's slide compares materials, check which figure of merit it uses and at what temperature. A bar chart of Baliga's figure says nothing about heat removal, switching loss, gate reliability, or the price of a wafer, and a device that wins on paper at 25 °C can lose at 150 °C. Ask for the measured device, at your temperature, in your circuit.",
job="Compound-semiconductor fabs and RF foundries employ device engineers who sit between materials and circuits. They grow and qualify epitaxial layers, tune the HEMT or HBT structure, run the reliability stresses of Chapter 17 on GaN and GaAs parts, and support their customers' PA and LNA designers with models and application notes. Much of the day is this chapter's kind of reasoning, a material constant turned into a device limit, together with the pulsed I-V and load-pull measurements that show whether a device meets it.",
practice=[
"Using the values of Section 25.1, compute the BFOM of AlN relative to silicon.",
"Compute the JFOM of diamond relative to silicon.",
"Ec rises roughly as Eg². Starting from silicon (1.12 eV, 0.3 MV/cm), predict Ec for GaN and compare it with 3.3 MV/cm.",
"Find the ideal drift-region Ron,sp of GaN at 600 V with the values of Section 25.1.",
"Why is Ga2O3 hard to use in a pn-junction device, and what limits its power density even as a Schottky diode?",
"An RF SOI switch stack must hold a 15 V peak swing with 20 % margin, with 2.5 V per transistor. How many transistors are needed?",
],
wyau=(["Eg, Ec, μ, vsat, and k are the five deciding constants.",
       "BFOM = εμEc³ for switches; JFOM = Ec vsat/2π for RF power.",
       "RF SOI for switches, SiGe for radar, GaN on SiC for watts, InP for the highest frequencies."],
      ["The Eg² scaling of critical field and the Eg⁶ scaling of BFOM.", "Doping limits of Ga2O3 and diamond."]),
)
CH[26] = dict(
title='Chapter 26. Lasers, VCSELs, APDs, SPADs, and Image Pixels',
open="A phone's face unlock fires a few milliwatts of infrared from a laser smaller than a grain of sand and times the return to tens of picoseconds. The laser, the detector, and the camera beside them are all junctions from Chapter 18, each given one more trick.",
where="Optical links, LiDAR and time-of-flight sensing, cameras, displays, fiber receivers, and solar power.",
blocks=[
('H2', '26.1 Choosing a wavelength'),
('P', "Chapter 18's rule λ (nm) ≈ 1240/E (eV), or λ (µm) ≈ 1.24/Eg in micrometers, ties every emitter and detector to its bandgap. GaN emits in the near ultraviolet at 1.24/3.4 = 0.365 µm; InGaN, with indium narrowing the gap, gives blue and green; AlGaInP gives red; GaAs and AlGaAs give the 850 nm of short fiber links and VCSELs; InGaAsP on InP gives 1310 and 1550 nm, the fiber windows of lowest dispersion and lowest loss. A detector absorbs only photons with energy above its gap. Silicon's 1.12 eV cuts off near 1.1 µm, so silicon cameras see the 850 and 940 nm illumination of face unlock but not 1550 nm. Germanium, whose direct gap is about 0.8 eV, and InGaAs lattice-matched to InP, at 0.75 eV, cover the fiber bands, and germanium grown on silicon brings 1310 and 1550 nm detection into silicon photonics processes."),
('WE', 'Worked Example 26.1', "Find the cutoff wavelengths of silicon, InGaAs (0.75 eV), and germanium's direct gap (0.80 eV), and the photon energies at 940 nm and 1550 nm. Which detectors see each wavelength?",
 "Si: 1.24/1.12 = 1.11 µm. InGaAs: 1.24/0.75 = 1.65 µm. Ge: 1.24/0.80 = 1.55 µm. At 940 nm, E = 1.24/0.94 = 1.32 eV, above all three gaps, so all three detect it; silicon absorbs weakly so close to its indirect edge, so a silicon detector at 940 nm needs a thick absorption region. At 1550 nm, E = 0.80 eV: silicon is transparent, InGaAs detects well, and germanium sits at its edge, which is why Ge-on-Si detectors use tensile strain, which narrows the direct gap, to stretch their response past 1550 nm."),
('H2', '26.2 From LED to laser'),
('P', "An LED emits by spontaneous recombination, in all directions and over a band of wavelengths, with output proportional to current. Its efficiency is the product of the internal quantum efficiency, the fraction of recombinations that emit, and the extraction efficiency, the fraction of photons that escape a high-index crystal. InGaN LEDs also show droop: efficiency falls at high current density, mostly because Auger recombination, a three-carrier process, grows as the cube of the carrier density. A lamp built from many LEDs at moderate current therefore beats a few driven hard."),
('P', "A laser adds two things to the junction: an optical cavity, mirrors that send photons back through the active region, and enough current that stimulated emission, in which a photon triggers a recombination that emits an identical photon, outruns the losses. Below the threshold current Ith the device is a dim LED. Above it, nearly every additional electron yields a coherent photon, and the output grows linearly with a slope efficiency η in watts per ampere. An edge-emitting laser has a cavity hundreds of micrometers long cleaved at the chip's edge, high power, and an elliptical beam. A vertical-cavity surface-emitting laser, VCSEL, has a cavity about a wavelength long between two Bragg mirrors grown above and below a few quantum wells. It emits from the top surface, has a threshold below a milliampere, can be tested on the wafer, and forms arrays, which is why VCSELs sit in data-center links at 850 nm and in phones' face unlock and proximity sensors."),
('EQ', 'P = η (I − Ith) for I > Ith;   ηd = η q/(hν) = η λ(µm)/1.24'),
('WE', 'Worked Example 26.2', "A VCSEL at 850 nm has Ith = 0.5 mA and a slope efficiency of 0.5 W/A (example values). Find the optical output at 6 mA, the differential quantum efficiency, and the wall-plug efficiency if the forward voltage is 2.0 V.",
 "P = 0.5 W/A × (6 − 0.5) mA = 2.75 mW. Each photon carries 1.24/0.85 = 1.46 eV, so ηd = 0.5/1.46 = 0.34: 34 % of the electrons above threshold produce a photon that leaves the device. The electrical input is 2.0 V × 6 mA = 12 mW, so the wall-plug efficiency is 2.75/12 = 23 %. The rest heats a structure a few micrometers across, and because the threshold rises with temperature, a VCSEL driver controls both bias and modulation current over temperature."),
('FIG', 'b3s_laser_apd.png', "Figure 26.1 — Top: the L-I curve of the VCSEL of Worked Example 26.2 against an LED example; the laser is dark below threshold and steep above it. Bottom: the APD excess noise factor F(M) of Section 26.3 for three ionization ratios k.", "Two panels: optical power against current with a laser line starting at 0.5 milliampere and a shallow LED line from zero, and excess noise factor against gain for silicon, indium gallium arsenide, and germanium."),
('H2', '26.3 Detectors with gain: PIN, APD, and SPAD'),
('P', "The PIN photodiode of Chapter 18 places an undoped intrinsic layer between p and n, so the depletion region, where absorbed photons become collected current, is wide and set by geometry rather than bias. Its gain is one: at most one electron per photon. An avalanche photodiode, APD, adds a high-field region where carriers gain enough energy to ionize the lattice, so each photoelectron becomes M electrons. The multiplication is random and therefore noisy. The excess noise factor F(M) depends on the ratio k of the hole and electron ionization rates, and a material in which only one carrier type ionizes, k near zero, is quiet. Silicon, with k near 0.02, makes the quietest APDs; InGaAs/InP APDs, with k near 0.4 to 0.5, are noisier but cover the fiber bands; germanium, with k near 0.9, is noisier still."),
('EQ', 'F(M) = k M + (1 − k)(2 − 1/M)'),
('WE', 'Worked Example 26.3', "Find F at M = 10 for silicon (k = 0.02) and for InGaAs/InP (k = 0.45). Explain why the optimum gain is higher for silicon when amplifier noise dominates without gain.",
 "Si: 0.02 × 10 + 0.98 × (2 − 0.1) = 0.2 + 1.86 = 2.06. InGaAs: 0.45 × 10 + 0.55 × 1.9 = 4.5 + 1.05 = 5.55. Gain lifts the signal above the amplifier's noise floor while the multiplied shot noise grows as M² F(M). The optimum M is where the shot-noise term catches up with the amplifier term; F grows far more slowly in silicon, so silicon APDs run at gains of 50 to 100 or more and InGaAs APDs nearer 10."),
('P', "Bias the avalanche region above its breakdown voltage and the diode has no stable gain at all: one carrier triggers a self-sustaining avalanche that a quench circuit must stop. That is the single-photon avalanche diode, SPAD, operated in Geiger mode. Each detected photon produces a large, fast pulse, after which the diode is blind for a dead time of tens of nanoseconds while it recharges. Its figures of merit are the photon detection efficiency; the dark count rate, avalanches triggered by thermal generation or tunneling, which rises steeply when hot; afterpulsing, from trapped carriers released late; and timing jitter, the spread of the delay from photon to pulse, often tens of picoseconds. SPAD arrays built in CMOS image-sensor processes are the receivers of most phone and many automotive LiDAR systems."),
('P', "Timing is the point. Light travels about 30 cm per nanosecond, so a reflection from a target at distance d returns after 2d/c, 6.67 ns per meter of range, and 67 ps corresponds to 1 cm. A SPAD LiDAR fires many laser pulses, builds a histogram of arrival times, and takes its peak, averaging the jitter and rejecting ambient light."),
('WE', 'Worked Example 26.4', "A SPAD time-of-flight sensor sees a return 20 ns after the laser pulse, with a timing jitter of 50 ps rms per detected photon. Find the distance, the single-photon range uncertainty, and the uncertainty after averaging 400 detected photons.",
 "d = c t/2 = 3×10⁸ × 20×10⁻⁹/2 = 3.0 m. One photon: σd = c σt/2 = 3×10⁸ × 50×10⁻¹²/2 = 7.5 mm. Averaging 400 photons divides by √400 = 20, giving 0.38 mm. Systematic errors, such as the pulse shape and the drift of the circuit's own delay, usually dominate long before that and are removed by calibration."),
('H2', '26.4 Image pixels and event pixels'),
('P', "A CMOS image sensor is an array of photodiodes, each with a few transistors. In the standard four-transistor pixel, light is collected in a pinned photodiode, a buried p-n junction designed to deplete fully, and a transfer gate moves the whole charge to a floating diffusion, a tiny capacitance whose voltage a source follower reads. The floating diffusion is sampled right after its reset and again after the transfer, and this correlated double sampling removes the reset noise; read noise of one to two electrons is common. The conversion gain is q/C of the floating diffusion: 1 fF gives 160 µV per electron."),
('P', "The pixel's limits are counted in electrons. The full-well capacity is the most charge the photodiode holds. Shot noise is the square root of the signal, so the best signal-to-noise ratio a pixel can reach is the square root of its full well, and its dynamic range is the full well over the read noise. A rolling shutter reads rows one after another, so fast motion skews; a global shutter stores every pixel's charge at the same instant in an in-pixel memory, at the cost of area and leakage."),
('WE', 'Worked Example 26.5', "A pixel has a full well of 6,000 electrons, a read noise of 2 electrons, and a floating-diffusion capacitance of 1.6 fF. Find the conversion gain, the dynamic range, the peak SNR, and the SNR at 100 electrons.",
 "q/C = 1.602×10⁻¹⁹/1.6×10⁻¹⁵ = 100 µV per electron. Dynamic range = 6000/2 = 3000, or 69.5 dB. Peak SNR = √6000 = 77.5, or 37.8 dB. At 100 electrons the shot noise is 10 electrons and the read noise 2, together √(100 + 4) = 10.2, so the SNR is 9.8, or 19.8 dB. In dim light the read noise hardly matters; the photon count does. A camera's low-light quality is bought with pixel area and quantum efficiency, not with a quieter amplifier alone."),
('P', "An event pixel, used in event-based vision sensors, takes no frames at all. Each pixel converts its photocurrent logarithmically, remembers the last value it reported, and emits an event, its address, a timestamp, and a sign, whenever the logarithm changes by more than a contrast threshold set by the designer, often in the range of 10 to 30 percent. A still scene produces almost no data; a moving edge produces events with microsecond timing. The logarithm gives a high dynamic range and the asynchronous readout a very low latency, at the cost of a data stream that frame-based vision code cannot read directly; Book 8 handles that stream."),
('H2', '26.5 Displays and solar: microLED, OLED, and the cell'),
('P', "MicroLEDs are the LEDs of Section 26.2 shrunk to a few micrometers and placed one per subpixel. Their brightness and lifetime beat OLED's, but two problems dominate. The sidewalls of a tiny mesa are a large fraction of its volume and recombine carriers without light, so efficiency falls as the LED shrinks; and transferring millions of dice per display with near-perfect yield is a manufacturing problem of its own. OLEDs are not crystals at all but thin organic films in which electrons and holes form excitons, bound pairs that emit when they recombine. Phosphorescent emitters harvest the triplet excitons, three quarters of the total, which fluorescent emitters waste; blue, the shortest-lived color, remains the hard one."),
('P', "Chapter 18's solar cell completes the family. The Shockley-Queisser efficiency limit for a single junction under sunlight is about 33 % near a gap of 1.34 eV; silicon, at 1.12 eV, reaches about 27 % in the best cells. Tandem cells, a perovskite or III-V top cell on silicon, pass the single-junction limit by giving each part of the spectrum its own gap."),
('KEY', "Key idea. The gap picks the wavelength. A cavity and a threshold make an LED a laser; gain and Geiger-mode bias make a photodiode an APD or a SPAD; a transfer gate and a floating diffusion make it a pixel. Count electrons and photons, and the noise follows."),
('P', "Light and its detectors are one family of sensors. Chapter 27 surveys the others."),
],
bench="Treat laser and LED drive currents as thermal problems. Threshold and slope efficiency both move with temperature, so a fixed current that gives the right power on the bench gives too much or too little in a hot enclosure; use the monitor photodiode or a temperature-compensated driver, and stay inside the eye-safety class the product was certified for. For SPAD receivers, measure the dark count rate at the highest operating temperature, not at room temperature, where it can be an order of magnitude lower.",
job="Optoelectronic test engineers characterize lasers, LEDs, and detectors with optical power meters, optical spectrum analyzers, integrating spheres, and picosecond timing systems; " + B9 + ", covers those instruments in its chapter \"Optical, Thermal, and EMC Precompliance Instruments\". In a LiDAR or camera company the same engineers own the eye-safety calculation and the temperature behavior of the emitter, and they are the first to see a dark count rate the data sheet did not warn about.",
practice=[
"Find the emission wavelength of a material with a 1.9 eV gap.",
"A laser has Ith = 12 mA and a slope efficiency of 0.3 W/A. Find the drive current for 5 mW of output.",
"Find the round-trip time of flight to a target 15 m away.",
"Find F(M) at M = 20 for k = 0.02.",
"A pixel has a full well of 10,000 electrons and a read noise of 1.5 electrons. Find its dynamic range and its peak SNR in decibels.",
"A floating diffusion has 2 fF. Find the conversion gain.",
],
wyau=(["λ (µm) ≈ 1.24/Eg; silicon stops near 1.1 µm.",
       "Laser output P = η(I − Ith) above threshold.",
       "Time of flight: 6.67 ns per meter of range, 67 ps per centimeter.",
       "Pixel SNR is √N in shot noise; dynamic range is full well over read noise."],
      ["McIntyre's excess-noise formula.", "Laser rate equations and threshold gain."]),
)
CH[27] = dict(
title='Chapter 27. Sensing Devices: MEMS, Hall, Gas, and Quantum',
open="A phone knows which way is down, how fast it is turning, where north is, how loud the room is, and sometimes how clean the air is. Each of those senses is a semiconductor device, and most of them are machined from the same silicon as the processor.",
where="Phones, wearables, vehicles, drones, industrial monitoring, air-quality and safety sensors, and the newest magnetometers.",
blocks=[
('H2', '27.1 MEMS: silicon that moves'),
('P', "Microelectromechanical systems, MEMS, are mechanical structures etched from silicon, polysilicon, or deposited films with the tools of Chapter 20. A MEMS accelerometer is a proof mass on silicon springs. An acceleration a displaces it by x = a/ω0², where ω0 = √(k/m) is its resonant frequency, and comb-shaped capacitor plates read the displacement as a change of capacitance. Below resonance the response is flat; above it the mass cannot follow. Gas damping sets the quality factor, and accelerometers are usually damped to a Q of about 0.5 to 1 so that a shock does not make them ring."),
('EQ', 'x = a / ω0²,   ΔC/C ≈ x / g0,   noise ≈ √(4 kB T ω0 / (m Q)) in m/s² per √Hz'),
('WE', 'Worked Example 27.1', "A MEMS accelerometer has a proof mass of 2 µg (2×10⁻⁹ kg), a resonance at 5 kHz, Q = 1, and capacitor gaps of 2 µm (example values). Find the displacement per g, the fractional capacitance change per g, and the thermomechanical noise floor at 300 K.",
 "ω0 = 2π × 5000 = 31,416 rad/s, so x = 9.81/31,416² = 9.94×10⁻⁹ m, about 10 nm, some 18 lattice constants of silicon. ΔC/C = 9.94 nm/2 µm = 0.50 %. Noise: √(4 × 1.38×10⁻²³ × 300 × 31,416/(2×10⁻⁹ × 1)) = √(2.60×10⁻⁷) = 5.1×10⁻⁴ m/s² per √Hz, about 52 µg/√Hz. The readout must resolve tiny fractions of a capacitance of order a picofarad, and that electronics, more than the silicon spring, is where most of the design effort goes."),
('FIG', 'b3s_mems.png', "Figure 27.1 — Proof-mass motion per g of the accelerometer of Worked Example 27.1 for three damping values. Below the 5 kHz resonance the response is flat at about 9.9 nm per g.", "Log-log plot of displacement per g against vibration frequency, flat at about 10 nanometers up to a few kilohertz, peaking near 5 kilohertz for Q of 5, and falling above resonance."),
('P', "A MEMS gyroscope drives a proof mass to vibrate along one axis and senses the Coriolis force when the device rotates: a mass moving with velocity v in a frame rotating at Ω feels an acceleration 2Ωv, perpendicular to both. The signal is small, the mechanical design is a pair of closely matched resonances, and the electronics is a synchronous detector locked to the drive."),
('WE', 'Worked Example 27.2', "A gyroscope's drive mode vibrates with a 10 µm amplitude at 20 kHz. Find the peak Coriolis acceleration at a rotation rate of 1 °/s.",
 "Peak velocity v = 2π × 20,000 × 10×10⁻⁶ = 1.26 m/s. Ω = 1 °/s = 0.01745 rad/s. a = 2Ωv = 2 × 0.01745 × 1.26 = 0.044 m/s², about 4.5 mg. Resolving 0.01 °/s means resolving 45 µg of Coriolis acceleration in the presence of the much larger drive motion, which is why gyroscopes need matched modes and synchronous detection."),
('P', "A MEMS microphone is a thin, compliant diaphragm about a millimeter across in front of a perforated rigid backplate. Sound pressure moves the diaphragm and changes the capacitance, which an ASIC in the same package biases, amplifies, and digitizes. Piezoelectric MEMS microphones use the AlN films of Chapter 21 instead of a biased capacitor."),
('H2', '27.2 Magnetic sensors: Hall and magnetoresistance'),
('P', "Part I used the Hall effect to measure carrier density. With a known density, the same effect measures the field: a plate carrying current I in a field B develops VH = I B/(n q t) across its width. Low carrier density and high mobility give a large, quiet signal, which is why discrete Hall sensors use InSb or GaAs, while Hall sensors integrated in CMOS accept silicon's smaller signal for on-chip amplification and offset cancellation. They measure current without contact, sense the position of magnets in motors and switches, and count wheel rotations in every car."),
('EQ', 'VH = I B / (n q t)'),
('WE', 'Worked Example 27.3', "A silicon Hall plate has n = 1×10¹⁶ cm⁻³ and a thickness of 10 µm. Find its current-related sensitivity and the Hall voltage at 1 mA in 10 mT.",
 "With n = 10²² m⁻³, 1/(n q t) = 1/(10²² × 1.602×10⁻¹⁹ × 10⁻⁵) = 62.4 V/(A·T). VH = 62.4 × 10⁻³ × 0.01 = 0.62 mV. The offset of a real plate, from mechanical stress and contact misalignment, can be as large as that signal. The spinning-current method, which rotates the current direction among the plate's contacts and averages the outputs, cancels most of it."),
('P', "Magnetoresistive sensors change resistance in a field. Anisotropic magnetoresistance, AMR, changes a permalloy film's resistance by a few percent. Giant and tunnel magnetoresistance, GMR and TMR, use thin magnetic multilayers and, for TMR, a tunnel barrier, the structures of hard-disk read heads and MRAM, to reach changes of tens to hundreds of percent. They are more sensitive than Hall plates and serve in electronic compasses and current sensors."),
('H2', '27.3 Gas sensors'),
('P', "A metal-oxide gas sensor is a heated film of tin oxide or tungsten oxide whose surface adsorbs oxygen, which captures electrons and leaves a depleted, resistive layer at each grain boundary. A reducing gas such as carbon monoxide or ethanol reacts with the adsorbed oxygen and returns the electrons, and the resistance falls, roughly as a power of the concentration. These sensors are inexpensive and sensitive, need a few hundred degrees and some milliwatts of heater power, drift with humidity and age, and respond to many gases at once, so their readings are better treated as trends than as concentrations."),
('P', "Nondispersive infrared, NDIR, sensors measure carbon dioxide by its absorption near 4.26 µm with an infrared source, an optical path, a filter, and a thermopile or photodiode detector. By the Beer-Lambert law the transmitted fraction is exp(−α c L) for a concentration c and path length L, so the reading is selective and stable, and calibration lasts years. Electrochemical cells, which oxidize or reduce the target gas at an electrode and measure the current, are the third family, used for toxic gases at low concentrations."),
('H2', '27.4 Quantum sensing with diamond'),
('P', "Some of the most sensitive solid-state magnetometers use a defect in diamond: a nitrogen atom next to a missing carbon atom, the nitrogen-vacancy, or NV, center. In its negative charge state the NV center has an electron spin whose ms = 0 and ms = ±1 sublevels are split by D = 2.87 GHz even without a field. Green laser light pumps the spin into ms = 0, and the ms = ±1 states fluoresce less brightly, so sweeping a microwave frequency while watching the red fluorescence shows a dip at each spin resonance: optically detected magnetic resonance. A field along the NV axis splits the two resonances by 2γB, with γ = 28 GHz/T, or 28 kHz per microtesla, and the field is read from that frequency."),
('EQ', 'f± = D ± γ B,   D = 2.87 GHz,   γ = 28 GHz/T'),
('WE', 'Worked Example 27.4', "An NV magnetometer sees its two resonances at 2.8644 and 2.8756 GHz. Find the field along the NV axis. What splitting does the Earth's field of about 50 µT produce at most?",
 "The splitting is 11.2 MHz = 2γB, so B = 11.2×10⁶/(2 × 28×10⁹) = 2.0×10⁻⁴ T = 200 µT. The center, 2.870 GHz, confirms D. The Earth's 50 µT along the axis splits the lines by 2 × 28 kHz/µT × 50 µT = 2.8 MHz. Resolving nanotesla fields means resolving tens of hertz on a 2.87 GHz line, which ensemble sensors with millions of NV centers can do; a single NV center gives nanometer-scale spatial resolution instead."),
('P', "Quantum sensing also includes atomic magnetometers and atomic clocks built on vapor cells, superconducting SQUIDs, and the SPADs of Chapter 26 counting single photons. What makes the NV center a semiconductor-device topic is that it works at room temperature in a solid, is made by implantation and annealing with the tools of Chapter 20, and is read out with a laser, a photodiode, and a microwave source."),
('KEY', "Key idea. A sensor turns a quantity into a device parameter: displacement into capacitance in MEMS, field into Hall voltage or resistance, gas into conductance or optical absorption, field into a spin-resonance frequency. The device sets the signal; the readout electronics usually sets the noise."),
('P', "Every device of this book, from the diode to the NV center, ends up on a chip or beside one. Chapter 28 asks how that chip is chosen and paid for."),
],
bench="Mount a MEMS sensor as part of the mechanics, because it is. Board stress from soldering, screws, or a flexing enclosure shifts the zero of an accelerometer or gyroscope, and a resonance of the board or housing near the sensor's bandwidth shows up as false motion. Follow the vendor's land pattern and keep-out rules, keep the part away from board-mounting holes, and measure the offset after assembly, not only on the bare part.",
job="Sensor companies hire MEMS process engineers, who make the moving structures and seal them in their vacuum or gas-filled cavities; mixed-signal designers, who build the capacitance-to-digital readout; test engineers, who shake, rotate, heat, and gas every part on calibration rigs; and applications engineers, who help customers with the board-stress and filtering problems of the From the Bench note. Sensor fusion, the firmware that combines accelerometer, gyroscope, and magnetometer readings into an orientation, belongs to Book 8.",
practice=[
"An accelerometer has a 2 kHz resonance. Find its displacement per g.",
"With the values of Worked Example 27.1 but a proof mass of 8 µg, find the noise floor.",
"Find the peak Coriolis acceleration at 100 °/s for the gyroscope of Worked Example 27.2.",
"A Hall sensor has a sensitivity of 100 V/(A·T). Find VH at 2 mA in 50 mT.",
"An NV center shows a resonance splitting of 5.6 MHz. Find the field along its axis.",
"An NDIR cell transmits 90 % at a concentration c1. What does it transmit at 3c1?",
],
wyau=(["x = a/ω0² for any spring-mass sensor.",
       "VH = IB/(nqt); spinning current removes the offset.",
       "NV center: 2.87 GHz at zero field, 28 kHz per µT per line."],
      ["Thermomechanical noise from the fluctuation-dissipation theorem.", "Surface chemistry of metal-oxide gas sensors."]),
)
CH[28] = dict(
title='Chapter 28. ASICs, FPGAs, and the Economics of a Chip',
open="A product needs a digital function that no catalog part offers. Program a microcontroller, configure an FPGA, or design a chip? The physics of this book says what each can do. The economics says which one you can afford, and the answer changes with every factor of ten in volume.",
where="Product planning, architecture reviews, startup business plans, and the career choice among board, FPGA, and chip design.",
blocks=[
('H2', '28.1 Three ways to build a function'),
('P', "A microcontroller is a Swiss Army knife: a processor, memory, and a drawer of peripherals, all fixed, all inexpensive, made by the hundred million. The function lives in firmware, which the user changes at will, and the limit is speed, because everything happens one instruction at a time. An FPGA is a box of Lego: a sea of lookup tables, flip-flops, memory blocks, and multipliers joined by programmable routing and configured by a bitstream into whatever circuit the designer describes. It runs truly in parallel at clock rates of hundreds of megahertz and can be rebuilt overnight, but each function costs much more silicon, power, and money than it would as a dedicated circuit, because the programmability itself occupies most of the die. An ASIC, an application-specific integrated circuit, is a custom machine: transistors laid out for one function only, the most efficient in power, speed, and unit cost, and the only one of the three that can put the analog, RF, sensor, and high-voltage devices of this book on the same die. It is also the only one that cannot be changed once made, and the only one with a large up-front cost."),
('H2', '28.2 From idea to silicon'),
('P', "An ASIC follows the path of this book in reverse. A specification becomes an architecture, the architecture becomes register-transfer-level code in a hardware description language, and simulation and formal checks verify it, often for longer than the design itself took. Synthesis maps the code onto the standard cells of the PDK of Chapter 11; placement and routing lay them out; timing, power, and signal-integrity analysis check the layout at the corners of Chapter 22; design-rule and layout-versus-schematic checks confirm the geometry. Analog and RF blocks are designed by hand with the device models of this book. The finished layout goes to the foundry as mask data, the tape-out, and wafers come back weeks to months later. Test, using the scan chains and built-in self-test inserted during design, separates good dice from bad; the yield of Chapter 20 decides how many good dice a wafer gives; and packaging, the subject of Book 7, finishes the part."),
('P', "The FPGA path shares the first half: the same hardware description languages and the same simulation, then synthesis onto the FPGA's fabric and a bitstream instead of masks. That shared front end is why many ASICs are prototyped on FPGAs first. Book 8, Firmware and Software for Electronics, teaches the languages in its chapter \"HDL: Software That Becomes Hardware\", with an inexpensive hobby FPGA board as the target."),
('H2', '28.3 NRE against unit cost'),
('P', "Non-recurring engineering, NRE, is everything paid once: engineering salaries, design-tool licenses, purchased IP blocks, the mask set, prototypes, and qualification. Unit cost is paid per part: silicon, package, test, and margin. The total cost per unit at a volume N is NRE/N plus the unit cost, and the cheapest option changes as N grows. The break-even volume between two options is the difference in NRE divided by the difference in unit cost."),
('EQ', 'cost per unit = NRE / N + unit cost;   N(break-even) = (NRE2 − NRE1) / (unit1 − unit2)'),
('WE', 'Worked Example 28.1', "Compare three ways to build a function, using example figures chosen for the arithmetic, not quoted prices: an FPGA design with an NRE of 0.2 million dollars and a unit cost of 25 dollars; an ASIC in a mature node with an NRE of 3 million and a unit cost of 2 dollars; and an ASIC in an advanced node with an NRE of 40 million and a unit cost of 1.50 dollars. Find the break-even volumes, and the cost per unit of each option at 10,000 and at 1 million units.",
 "FPGA against mature ASIC: (3 − 0.2) million/(25 − 2) = 121,700 units. Mature against advanced ASIC: (40 − 3) million/(2 − 1.5) = 74 million units. At 10,000 units the FPGA costs 0.2 million/10⁴ + 25 = 45 dollars per unit, the mature ASIC 300 + 2 = 302, and the advanced one 4,000 + 1.50 = 4,001.50. At 1 million units: FPGA 25.20, mature ASIC 5.00, advanced ASIC 41.50. The FPGA wins small volumes, the mature-node ASIC wins from about 122,000 units, and the advanced node pays off on cost alone only beyond tens of millions (Figure 28.1); it is usually chosen for performance or power the mature node cannot deliver. If an off-the-shelf microcontroller can do the job at all, with an NRE that is mostly firmware time and a unit cost of a few dollars, it beats all three at almost any volume. The custom options earn their place only where it cannot."),
('FIG', 'b3s_nre.png', "Figure 28.1 — Cost per unit including NRE against volume, for the example figures of Worked Example 28.1. On cost alone the FPGA wins below about 122,000 units, the mature-node ASIC above it, and the advanced node only beyond about 74 million.", "Log-log plot of cost per unit against units shipped for three options, each falling from a high value and flattening at its unit cost, with crossover lines near 122 thousand and 74 million units."),
('P', "Real figures vary by orders of magnitude with the node, the design size, and how much IP is bought rather than written; the mask set alone for an advanced node costs more than a whole mature-node project. Two refinements change the picture. Multi-project wafers share one mask set among many designs, so a prototype run costs a small fraction of a full mask set. Structured approaches, an existing chip with a configurable layer or a chiplet added to a standard package, sit between the FPGA and full custom."),
('P', "For students and hobbyists, the shared shuttle has gone further. Programs such as Tiny Tapeout collect many small designs onto one chip, built on open process design kits such as those for the SkyWater 130 nm, IHP 130 nm SiGe BiCMOS, and GlobalFoundries 180 nm processes, and charge according to the area a design occupies. Prices and schedules change from run to run, so check the program's own pages. The point is that a real ASIC, with your own logic or a small analog block on it, is now within reach of a classroom."),
('H2', '28.4 Analog, mixed-signal, and RF ASICs'),
('P', "Most of the devices in this book cannot be configured into FPGA fabric. A sensor interface, a battery charger, a power-management unit, an RF transceiver, or a motor driver needs the matched pairs of Chapter 12, the op-amps of Chapter 13, the noise budget of Chapter 14, the high-voltage devices of Chapter 19, or the MEMS of Chapter 27 on the same die as its logic, and that is the analog and RF ASIC niche. Such chips are often built in mature nodes, from 180 nm down to 40 nm and below, because analog precision, high voltage, and low leakage do not improve as fast as logic density, and because the NRE is far lower. Their design is still largely manual, which is why experienced analog and RF designers remain scarce."),
('H2', '28.5 Who does what'),
('P', "A chip project is a team effort. Architects define the function and partition it. RTL designers write the hardware description, and verification engineers, often more numerous, build the testbenches and coverage that try to break it. Physical-design engineers synthesize, place, route, and close timing. Analog and RF designers build the blocks no synthesis tool can, and layout engineers draw them. Design-for-test engineers insert the scan and self-test structures, and test engineers write the production programs. Device and modeling engineers, met in Chapter 23, supply the models; product and yield engineers take the chip into production and keep it there; applications engineers help customers use it. A board designer, an FPGA engineer, and a chip designer start from the same physics and the same digital logic, and many careers move among the three."),
('KEY', "Key idea. Microcontroller, FPGA, and ASIC trade flexibility for efficiency. Cost per unit is NRE/N plus unit cost, so the right choice depends on volume, and an ASIC is also the only way to put this book's analog, RF, power, and sensor devices on the same die as the logic."),
('P', "That closes the path from a crystal that can be talked into conducting to a chip someone decides to pay for. The later books of the series take these devices and chips as given and build radios, modems, power converters, and packages from them; when they need a device's behavior, it is this book's physics, read through a PDK or a fitted model."),
],
bench="Before committing to an ASIC, prototype the digital part on an FPGA and the analog parts on a test chip or an evaluation board, and write down the volume forecast that justifies the NRE. Forecasts are usually optimistic, so run the break-even arithmetic at half and at a quarter of the forecast. If the ASIC still wins, it is probably the right decision; if it wins only at the forecast, the FPGA or a catalog part is the safer bet.",
job="Chip companies hire for every role of Section 28.5, and the entry points differ. Verification and physical design take many graduates with strong digital and scripting skills. Analog and RF design favors those who enjoyed Parts II to IV of this book and are patient with both the simulator and the bench. Test and product engineering suits people who like data, statistics, and the factory floor, and applications engineering those who like customers and circuits equally. FPGA design is the most accessible start, since a board costs little and the vendors' tools have free editions, and its skills carry straight into ASIC front-end work.",
practice=[
"Option A has an NRE of 0.5 million dollars and a unit cost of 12 dollars; option B has an NRE of 2.5 million and a unit cost of 4 dollars. Find the break-even volume.",
"With the figures of Worked Example 28.1, find the cost per unit of each option at 100,000 units.",
"A respin raises the mature ASIC's NRE in Worked Example 28.1 by 50 %. Find the new break-even volume against the FPGA.",
"Give two reasons why an analog or RF ASIC is often built in a mature node.",
"Why are many ASICs prototyped on an FPGA first?",
"An FPGA needs about twenty times the silicon area of an ASIC for the same logic. Why can it still be the economical choice?",
],
wyau=(["Cost per unit = NRE/N + unit cost; break-even = ΔNRE/Δ(unit cost).",
       "Swiss Army knife, Lego, custom machine: microcontroller, FPGA, ASIC.",
       "Prototype on an FPGA; share masks on a multi-project wafer."],
      ["Detailed mask-cost and die-cost models.", "The ASIC design flow tool by tool."]),
)
